### Auth — log in as acme's seeded admin and capture the token.
### Every request below sends it as a bearer token. There is no
### X-Tenant-Id header any more: the tenant comes from the token's
### signed tenant_id claim, so a client can no longer choose it.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/token
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "tenantId": "acme",
  "email": "admin@acme.test",
  "password": "subscriptionhub"
}

> {%
    client.global.set("acmeToken", response.body.token);
%}


### Ping — happy path (known tenant)
# Verifies the tenant filter resolves a known tenant and lets the request through.
GET {{baseUrl}}/ping
Authorization: Bearer {{acmeToken}}

### Ping — no token (expects 401 UNAUTHENTICATED)
# There is no "missing tenant" case any more: with the tenant carried in a
# signed claim, the absence of a tenant is simply the absence of a token,
# and Spring Security rejects it before any tenant logic runs.
GET {{baseUrl}}/ping

### Ping — a token that isn't one (expects 401)
# Nothing can be forged without the signing secret, so an unparseable or
# tampered token is rejected exactly like no token at all.
GET {{baseUrl}}/ping
Authorization: Bearer not.a.real.token

### Inspect the Problem+JSON body of a 401
# Confirms "requestId" is present and matches the requestId in the server
# logs (MDC). Worth checking specifically: Spring Security rejects inside
# the filter chain, before any controller, so this body exists only because
# SecurityProblemHandler renders it - the framework default is an empty 401.
GET {{baseUrl}}/ping

### OpenAPI JSON - public group
GET {{baseUrl}}/v3/api-docs

### Swagger UI
GET {{baseUrl}}/swagger-ui/index.html

### Example: product list
# The catalog API exists now; kept here as the smallest example of an
# authenticated, tenant-scoped call.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/products
Authorization: Bearer {{acmeToken}}
